    "body": "> It seems to make sense that if we allow this for `<select>` we should allow this for `<datalist>`.\n\nYes, if people are asking for it like they are for select, which I could imagine happening after we implement customizable combobox.\n\n> Does that get messy? \n\nI don't see any reason that doing this for datalist would be any harder than doing it for select.\n\n> What about `<fieldset disabled>`, should that mark slotted elements disabled?\n\nI haven't seen this asked for before. I wonder if custom element authors are using fieldset in their shadowroot and slotting in form-associated elements.\n\n> What about `<form>` itself (see [#10220](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/10220))? I imagine `<form>` would be the riskiest in terms of web compatibility (especially concerning would be FACE, I guess?).\n\nMaking a custom element behave like a form seems like a good solution there, just like form-associated custom elements solves a similar problem without needing to make the browser track stuff in the flat tree.\n\nForm-associated custom elements enable custom elements to have form-associated elements in their shadowroot and pass through their state to an ancestor form element, but this case doesn't involve slotting:\n```html\n<form>\n  <x-input>\n    #shadow-root\n      <input>\n```\nSolving this issue with select in a way that doesn't involve slotting would be nice, but I'm struggling to find an appealing way to do it. Maybe we could make custom elements that behave like select elements and option elements, but that just sounds like custom builtins. How else can we associate the select element and option elements in an example like this?\n```html\n<x-select>\n  #shadow-root\n    <select>\n      <slot>\n  <x-option>\n    #shadow-root\n      <option>\n```\nMaybe custom builtins aren't powerful enough for this use case since they don't have a shadowroot available for the custom element to put whatever it wants in there, but this is what I'd imagine it looks like:\n```html\n<select is=x-select>\n  <option is=x-option>\n```\n\nIn general, I'm interested in making the select element support slotted option elements because multiple authors are asking for it and because other cases like forms and FACE have different types of solutions that work well.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
